# call with no region_mm is.
test_reference_measurement.gd assertions=26

# Fasteners: a 60 x 60 x 1.6 board posed away from the origin AND TURNED (a
# yaw and a lean, so the screw axis is nowhere near world z) with five 3.4 mm
# through holes and a blocker part standing over the third, and a shell of six
# bosses with 2.4 mm pilot bores — one coaxial, one 0.5 mm off and 2 degrees
# tilted, one under the blocker, one parked away from every hole, one standing
# 1.2 mm clear of the board with a rib of the SHELL bridging that gap at
# MID-RADIUS of the shank, where neither the axis ray nor the outer ring can
# see it and only a fan over the whole disc can, and
# one under a hole whose seat plane is milled away on one side of its axis.
# The exact B-Rep read is pinned by worker/tests/test_features.py (the cad GD
# harness has no worker) and the hole measurement by test_mesh_measurement.gd;
# everything between them is real here. Pins the one-to-one pairing (five
# holes, five seats, nothing claimed twice, the loose bore listed), an M3
# passing on all four counts with its 6.4 mm engagement and its seat on the
# board's top face in both frames, the tilted boss failing on the ISO 1101 zone
# alone with the offset measured at BOTH ends of the engaged length and the ISO
# 273 medium allowance behind it, the blocked screw naming the node and the
# point in the blocker's own height while its coaxiality stays clean, the
# milled seat reporting half its own 29-ray ring unsupported while the screw
# still passes (the seat fraction is advisory, not graded), the fitted axis
# agreeing with the B-Rep one inside 0.01 mm / 0.05 degrees (the measurement
# that licenses the fallback) with the radius difference reported and not
# graded, the status line naming the first failure, a size ISO 273 does not
# tabulate coming back ungraded — not failed — until the caller states the
# clearance hole, a hole record with no thickness being a named row rather than
# a silent seat at the plate's centre, and three refusals that report pass
# FALSE rather than an empty success.
# The fixture also carries a 355-degree GROOVE of a supported diameter sitting
# exactly on hole 2's axis — nearer it than boss B, which is half a millimetre
# off — so a check that pairs on geometry alone hands hole 2 to a surface that
# is open on one side and loses B's row entirely. Only a cylinder that sweeps
# the whole turn is a bore; the groove is listed as a partial cylinder with
# its sweep. And the worker's full-turn extent is read in angular bins, so every bore carries an
# error bar and engagement is graded on the bite MINUS it.
# The request asks the worker for the partial surfaces TOO (a worker-side
# filter would make the reply's own "partial cylinder" listing unreachable),
# the stand-in honours closed_only so that path is proven, and the closed rule
# is the WHOLE turn, applied from the threshold the worker states on every row
# rather than from a constant of the panel's own: the 355-degree groove on
# hole 2's axis — nearer it than boss B — is not a bore.
# A check answers in the poses it was CALLED with: the panel is re-posed while
# one check waits for the worker and a second is started on the new pose, and
# neither answers in the other's frame. And when the reference COLLIDERS are
# rebuilt in that same gap the check comes back STALE with no rows at all —
# the holes it was handed were measured at the old pose by a verb this module
# does not run, so there is nothing fresh to pair against and new geometry may
# not be measured against old holes. An extent the kernel could not read
# exactly is reported as ungraded rather than passed. And a check that returns
# before casting a ray reports its own zero cost, not the last check's. And
# the re-pose the panel actually performs — the same record with its pose
# rewritten IN PLACE, no collider rebuilt — comes back stale too, which only a
# snapshot that is a COPY of the records can notice. And the window BEFORE a
# check starts: a pose rewritten and the colliders not yet rebuilt, with
# nothing changing during the wait — the check compares what the gauge was
# built from with the records it holds and comes back stale (colliders behind
# poses) rather than casting old rays and reporting them in the new frame.
# Seating is a DEPTH question with a depth tolerance: hole 2 is counterbored
# 0.3 mm — a complete annular floor inside the ring's 0.5 mm lateral pitch —
# and must read as unsupported with the gap measured, not as a seat.
# And the shank fan does not stop at the mouth: the same rays carry on down
# the ENGAGED bore, where only the bore's own wall may be met — a separate
# check on a one-boss shell with a web across its bore 2 mm below the mouth
# reports the path blocked by the solid inside the bore, at the web's depth.
# Only the SOLID has a bore wall: a reference sleeve whose top face sits at
# the bore radius inside the engaged span — met by a ring of rays at exactly
# that radius — is an obstruction naming the sleeve. And the head fan sees
# the solid too: an annular bridge of the shell standing on the seat, outside
# the shank and inside the head, fails head_seat_clear naming the solid while
# the shank path and the seat ring are untouched. The wall is a FACE, not a
# radius: a thin shelf spanning R-0.05..R at depth, met by a ray within the
# wall tolerance of the bore radius, is an obstruction because its normal is
# axial. And a screw longer than its blind bore is deep BOTTOMS: the fan runs
# to the tip, meets the floor at the extent end, and the row fails with the
# tip, the floor and the excess while the 8 mm screw in the same bore does not.
# 63 assertions = 4 fixture + 4 envelope (+1: the disc's radial AND angular
# spacing, with the rays it placed counted) + 5 pairing (+1: the groove is not
# paired and is named, +1: partial surfaces are asked for and a one-bin-short
# bore is still a bore) + 8 A (+1: engagement measured on the
# full-circumference extent, +1: graded on that extent minus its error bar)
