# A boss whose flat top is coplanar with the board's underside is a designed
# contact, not interference. The fixture is a GRID-TRIANGULATED plate over a
# cylinder rim — the box-on-a-floor flush fixture has two triangles a face and
# no edge running over the rim, which is why it never caught this — and the
# control is the same boss driven 0.5 mm into the plate. The coplanarity is
# asked twice, exact and float-noisy (1e-5 mm, inside TOUCH_EPSILON_MM): only
# the noisy one reproduces the report, because exact coplanarity is already
# cleared by the per-crossing parity tests, so it is the noisy one that turns
# red when the contact-run gate is removed.
# The same contact is then asked with the ROLES SWAPPED — the boss mounted as
# the reference, the plate evaluated as the solid — because each cast
# direction carries its own copy of the contact-run gate and only the swap
# puts the plate's dense underside edges on the reference-cast path, where
# _drop_contact_runs is the sole reason they are cleared. Its lifts bracket
# TOUCH_EPSILON_MM itself: 9e-5 mm in is still a contact, 2e-4 mm in is not.
# The last unswapped case is the contact as a MODELLER delivers it: the same
# plate tilted five microradians, so its underside is coplanar with the boss's
# top to within float noise rather than exactly. Its edges then enter the boss
# through the rim wall and leave through the TOP FACE, and that top-face hit
# is discarded — the edge straddles it by far less than the epsilon. Unless
# the discarded hit is still handed to the contact-run rule as a run BOUNDARY,
# the surviving rim crossing measures its run out to the far end of the
# triangle, through the air past the boss, and a designed contact is reported.
# The swapped set ends the same way, and there the tilt is the FALSIFIER for
# the reference leg's own keep: an exactly coplanar swapped case has both ends
# of a plate chord penetrating the rim, so its run is bounded either way, while
# the tilted one needs the discarded top-face hit as its far bound.
# The boss is then asked again BORED, because a seating face carries a screw:
# the pilot's mouth is in the contact plane, so a chord over it lies in the
# plane and in no face, and a gate that only asks whether a run lies in a face
# reports the whole flush fit. Its control is the same half-millimetre bite,
# bored the same way — a rule that cleared the mouth by clearing everything
# coplanar would clear that too.
# 19 assertions = 2 fixture premises (the plate really is grid-triangulated;
# its underside edges really do cross the rim) + 1 collider built + 2 the
# flush boss reports nothing, exactly coplanar and a hundred-thousandth of a
# millimetre out + 2 the bitten boss reports the board's node and, because no
# single edge spans a half-millimetre disc, no depth with it + 1 the depth
# control (the same boss driven clean through the board reports the board's
# thickness) + 4 bored (1 the plate's edges really do cut the bore's rim too,
# 2 the bored boss is a contact at both flush lifts, 1 the bored boss bitten
# half a millimetre in is still interference) + 1 the tilted board is still a
# contact + 7 swapped (1 the boss became the one reference collider, 2 the two
# lifts inside the CONTACT TOLERANCE are contacts, 3 the two lifts past it and
# the half-millimetre bite are interference on the boss's node, 1 the tilted
# plate over an exactly coplanar boss is a contact on the reference-cast leg).
# The swapped brackets moved from either side of the touch epsilon to either
# side of expected_contacts.gd's CONTACT_TOLERANCE_MM (bug 01a079f9f8f4):
# 2e-4 mm is the precision the hit positions arrive in and is now a contact,
# and 0.02 mm was ADDED beside the 0.05 mm control so the tolerance cannot
# drift across that gap unseen — one more assertion than before.
test_flush_contact.gd assertions=20

# The coaxial cousin of the case above: a clearance bore seated on a plate
